      "math_academy_id": "q-35131",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(2u)\\cdot v?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, multiplying each component of $u$ by $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u)\\cdot v & =2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4+10+2+0 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
